# Node.js Development

## Modern JavaScript

- Use `const` by default; use `let` only when reassignment is required
- Never use `var`
- Prefer ES modules (`import` / `export`) when the project supports them
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) where appropriate

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD
const user = await getUser();
const city = user?.address?.city ?? "Unknown";
```

## Async Programming

- Use `async/await` only — avoid `.then()` / `.catch()` chains
- Handle asynchronous errors using `try/catch`
- Never block the event loop with synchronous operations unless absolutely necessary

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD
async function getUsers() {
  try {
    return await User.find();
  } catch (err) {
    throw err;
  }
}
```

## Project Structure

- Keep modules focused on a single responsibility
- Separate routes, controllers, services, models, middleware, and utilities
- Avoid circular dependencies
- Reuse utility functions instead of duplicating code

## Code Quality

- Write small, readable, and reusable functions
- Prefer early returns over deeply nested conditionals
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Keep files organized and easy to navigate

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD
if (!user) {
  return null;
}

return user;
```

## Error Handling

- Throw meaningful Error objects
- Never silently ignore exceptions
- Log errors with sufficient context
- Return user-friendly messages without exposing internal implementation

```javascript
throw new Error("Invalid user ID");
```

## Environment Variables

- Store secrets in `.env`
- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, passwords, or credentials
- Document all required variables in `.env.example`

```env
PORT=5000
MONGODB_URI=
JWT_SECRET=
```

## Security

- Validate and sanitize all external input
- Never trust client data
- Use environment variables for sensitive configuration
- Avoid exposing stack traces in production

## Performance

- Prefer asynchronous filesystem APIs
- Avoid unnecessary loops and repeated database calls
- Cache expensive computations when appropriate
- Stream large files instead of loading them fully into memory

## Logging

- Log important application events
- Include timestamps and context in logs
- Never log passwords, authentication tokens, or sensitive user data

## Testing

- Write unit tests for reusable modules
- Mock external services during testing
- Keep business logic easy to test by avoiding unnecessary side effects